Android App Links — not yet live, deliberately.

Serving a well-known file with a wrong fingerprint makes Android fail verification silently,
which is worse than not serving one. So this is a template, not a served file.

To turn it on:
  1. Get the SHA-256 signing fingerprint. For a Play-signed app it is the one in
     Play Console -> Release -> Setup -> App integrity -> App signing key certificate.
     It is not a secret. Do not read it out of the local keystore.
  2. Put it below in place of PLACEHOLDER, uppercase hex with colons.
  3. Move this file to .well-known/assetlinks.json (that exact name, no extension change),
     and confirm it serves as application/json with no redirect.
  4. Add the https intent-filter with android:autoVerify="true" to the app's manifest.
     MainActivity already routes on the URI path, so nothing else needs to change.

[{
  "relation": ["delegate_permission/common.handle_all_urls"],
  "target": {
    "namespace": "android_app",
    "package_name": "com.edifice.app",
    "sha256_cert_fingerprints": ["PLACEHOLDER"]
  }
}]
